  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe warm up continues Wednesday as the upper ridge continues \ndrifting east into the Ohio Valley.  This brings deep southwest flow \nto the Mid Mississippi Valley, with 850mb temps rising to 18C-20C \naccording to the deterministic GFS and ECMWF.  This agrees well with \n850mb temps forecast by the LREF which is only showing around 1 \ndegree difference between the 25th and 75th percentile. 18C-20C at \n850mb would mix down to 88F-91F at the surface Wednesday afternoon, \nwhich is very close to the NBM max T forecast.  Cooler temperatures in \nthe low 80s are showing up across northern Missouri and west central \nIllinois, but this can be explained by more extensive cloud cover \nahead of the next upstream trough.\n\nThe trough will pick up the remnants of hurricane Polo on Tuesday as \nit drifts across the Desert Southwest, and latest guidance has it \nbeginning to impact our forecast area Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning.  The incorporation of tropical moisture from Polo brings \nthe possibility of heavy rain to the area as the trough drifts \nthrough the Plains into the Mississippi Valley on Thursday and \nFriday.  LREF mean precipitable water values rise to around 2 inches \nand forecast soundings show warm cloud depths in excess of 12kft \nThursday into Friday.  With this in mind, there will be a threat for \nlocally heavy rain late Wednesday night through Friday night. \nHowever, QPF guidance has trended lower over the past couple of runs \nas the trough and surface front are more progressive through the Mid \nMississippi Valley.  LREF mean QPF totals through Saturday are in \nthe 1.25 to 1.75 inch range, and even the 90th percentile tops out \nat about 3.30 inches over a period of 48-56 hours.  That much rain \nover a 2+ day period shouldn't cause too many problems, but some \nlocalized flooding will be possible in flood-prone locations if \nthere's training or we ultimately get more rain than expected. \nTemperatures cool off for the end of the week into the weekend, \nfirst due to clouds and precipitation on Thursday and Friday. \nCurrent guidance then pushes the cold front through the forecast \narea Friday night which will bring a cooler and drier airmass into \nthe Midwest for the weekend.  Temperature IQRs in the LREF are \naround 8 degrees Saturday and Sunday, but even the 75th percentile \nhigh temperatures are a degree or two below normal.  This leads to \nhigh confidence in cooler weather at the end of the forecast period.",
